---
import DocsNav from "../components/DocsNav.astro";
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import {
  evidenceHighlights,
  evidenceLevels,
  performanceEvidenceUrl,
  pagedPrefillOptimizationEvidenceUrl,
  raggedPrefillOptimizationEvidenceUrl,
  performanceRows,
  repositoryUrl,
} from "../data/site";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Evidence | Oxide Infer"
  description="Bounded correctness and performance records for Oxide Infer providers."
  active="Evidence"
>
  <header class="page-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Evidence</p>
    <h1>Each result keeps its boundary.</h1>
    <p>
      A result fixes the source, hardware, contract, baseline, method, and
      excluded claims.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="docs-layout">
    <DocsNav active="Evidence" />
    <article class="prose">
      <h2>Evidence levels</h2>
      <div class="evidence-level-grid">
        {evidenceLevels.map((item) => (
          <div class="evidence-level-card">
            <span>{item.level}</span>
            <div><h3>{item.name}</h3><p>{item.detail}</p></div>
            <small>{item.status}</small>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <h2>Current evidence status</h2>
      <table class="evidence-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Result</th><th>Direction</th><th>Contract</th><th>Boundary</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {evidenceHighlights.map((item) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{item.value}</td>
              <td>{item.label}</td>
              <td>{item.detail}</td>
              <td>{item.sample}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Current performance baseline</h2>
      <p>
        Four representative rows are shown below. The complete record contains
        14 stable BF16 attention shapes, both provider orders, and all 2,800
        latency samples.
      </p>
      <table class="evidence-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Contract</th><th>Oxide</th><th>FlashInfer 0.6.17</th><th>Result</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {performanceRows.map((item) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{item.name}<br /><small>{item.shape}</small></td>
              <td>{item.oxideUs} µs</td>
              <td>{item.flashinferUs} µs</td>
              <td>{item.result}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="doc-note">
        <strong>Operator timing, not a serving claim.</strong>
        <p>
          Measured on one NVIDIA H20 using matched eager-provider CUDA-event
          timing. The 14-shape baseline is retained, while the long paged- and
          ragged-GQA4 rows are refreshed from source-bound optimization records.
        </p>
        <a href={performanceEvidenceUrl} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
          Open the current comparison record and raw samples
        </a>
      </div>

      <div class="doc-note">
        <strong>Ragged GQA4 pipelines K and V tile loads.</strong>
        <p>
          The source-bound cohort moves from 39.42 to 37.04 µs. In the
          separate provider-comparison cohort, Oxide measures 36.94 µs and
          FlashInfer 0.6.17 measures 21.93 µs, a 1.68× gap.
        </p>
        <a href={raggedPrefillOptimizationEvidenceUrl} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
          Optimized ragged-GQA4 performance record
        </a>
      </div>

      <div class="doc-note">
        <strong>Current-source R1 device qualification is complete.</strong>
        <p>
          The recorded H20 qualification binds clean source{" "}
          <code>477b47a</code>, strict CUDA Clippy, seven release{" "}
          <code>sm_90a</code> lab tests, all nine permanent runners, 89 passing
          case lines, and 12 named Graph cases.
        </p>
        <p>
          All 36 Compute Sanitizer cells pass without filters or suppressions.
          Performance is published separately above. Real-model engine
          execution, serving, and other GPUs remain outside the R1 record.
        </p>
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/docs/results/h20-r1-phase2-qualification-477b47a-20260812.json`}>
          Current-source R1 phase 2 record
        </a>
      </div>

      <div class="doc-note">
        <strong>Paged prefill has four explicit algorithms.</strong>
        <p>
          The caller selects direct, eight-warp, sixteen-warp, or tiled GQA4
          execution. Tiled GQA4 uses a caller-owned F32 partial workspace.
        </p>
        <p>
          The current paged-GQA4 path is 18.45% lower latency than source
          <code>49290b5</code> in a separate source cohort. In the matched
          provider cohort, Oxide measures 46.60 µs and FlashInfer 23.21 µs, a
          2.01× gap.
        </p>
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/docs/results/h20-bf16-paged-prefill-token-parallel-correctness-20260807.json`}>
          Historical token-parallel correctness record
        </a>
        <span> · </span>
        <a href={pagedPrefillOptimizationEvidenceUrl} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
          Optimized paged-GQA4 performance record
        </a>
      </div>

      <div class="doc-note">
        <strong>Engine interop is a simulated boundary.</strong>
        <p>
          The phase 2 device gate qualifies external pointers, the event-bridge
          path, zero adapter D2D copies, recovery, and all four sanitizer tools
          for direct single and HND paged decode. It does not run a model. A
          mistral.rs provider hit and model-output comparison remain open.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="doc-note">
        <strong>Fused append now uses a checked three-command path.</strong>
        <p>
          One validator builds a cache-bound append map. The mapped kernel then
          rotates Q and K and writes private K/V pages. A device-status copy
          reports semantic rejection at completion.
        </p>
        <p>
          The 2026-08-06 append records measured the earlier single-kernel path
          without exclusive-page ownership or typed status. Phase 2 qualifies
          the current path's declared correctness, valid-output and rejection
          Graph cases, and sanitizer matrix, but not current performance.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Current exclusions</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>No result proves model or serving speedup.</li>
        <li>Eager provider timing is not isolated kernel timing.</li>
        <li>Graph results cover fixed addresses and named paths only.</li>
        <li>Recorded graph node counts are declarations, not driver enumeration.</li>
        <li>H20 results do not qualify other GPU architectures.</li>
        <li>One shape does not establish a universal provider winner.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Read the complete <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/docs/results/README.md`}>evidence index</a>
        {" "}for source hashes, fixtures, raw samples, and exclusions.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
